title: PR-4 kernel log landed; M1.2 p99 falsifier trips at 1e6 rows (anchor rename + WAL fsync suspected)
summary: |-
  PR-4 (cb56927b) adds #kernel-log and the symbol drift CI. Details:
  - node:sqlite chain with segments and rotation;
  - a Signer seam with a software Ed25519 kernel key and domain-separated signatures;
  - a file head anchor written by atomic rename, plus a keystore anchor stub;
  - verify catches flip, forge, truncate and reorder; crash and torn-tail recovery;
  - per-kind default labels and the §9.3 read projection;
  - oracles G-LOG-01..04 and 06..09, 130 kernel tests;
  - the symbols-drift test: @symbol header tags in src/kernel/** must map to .purpose symbols and Appendix A or an allowlist with reasons (12 entries).

  Perf at 10k rows: p50 0.17 ms, p99 0.3-0.9 ms, footprint 35 MB (pass). At 1e6 rows: p99 7.4 ms (FAILS the 5 ms falsifier); footprint is 74 MB after a checkpoint fix (it was 156 MB). About 2.3% of appends take around 7.5 ms. Raw SQLite and the head rename are each clean alone. Hypothesis: the WAL fsync flushes the APFS metadata from the per-commit anchor rename. The fix would write the anchor less often, a §3.2 policy change ('written after every commit') needing a founder decision with Aegis input, because an un-anchored tail can be truncated undetected.

  Builder resolutions to confirm in v3.8:
  - only daemon/socket/schedule events may be unsigned;
  - the anchor is not fsynced per commit (F_FULLFSYNC ~5 ms);
  - durability defaults to process-crash, with power-loss opt-in;
  - the appender caps audience at private;
  - agent/voice readers are UNSUPPORTED for now.
symbols_touched:
